{"uncertainty": 0.15, "outcome": "rtl_bug", "artifacts": [{"kind": "root_cause_analysis", "title": "uart_tx regression failure: holding register reloaded by a start pulse while busy", "summary": "Requirement: [requirement:req-d10c4df532]. Inputs: the approved RTL [artifact:prj-a0b3e1ca75.design-under-investigation.a1], its review record [evidence:prj-a0b3e1ca75.design-under-investigation.e1], the triage session [evidence:prj-a0b3e1ca75.triage.e1] / [run:run-0001], and the upstream reproduction [evidence:prj-a0b3e1ca75.triage.e2] / [run:run-0002]. CONCLUSION: rtl_bug. Mechanism, from reading the RTL: before the case statement, uart_tx has the unconditional statement 'if (start) hold <= data;'. It runs in every state, including START, DATA and STOP, while busy is high. The DATA state reads each next bit from hold (tx <= hold[bit_idx + 3'd1]). So a start pulse during a frame overwrites hold, and the rest of that frame carries bits from the new byte. The module header states the intended behaviour: 'A start pulse while a frame is in flight is ignored.' The busy-time reload contradicts that statement. Fix direction, not yet implemented or reviewed: remove the unconditional load and keep only the load inside the IDLE branch. RANKED HYPOTHESES, each with the evidence that would refute it: (H1, accepted) RTL bug, the busy-time reload of hold. Refuted if the attached testbench gets the correct byte from a frame that receives a mid-frame start pulse, or if its failing check is any check other than the 'frame 2 data' check. (H2, rejected) Testbench bug, for example sampling at the wrong bit-time or a badly placed stimulus pulse. Refuted, by design of the attached testbench, if frame 1 passes: frame 1 uses the same receiver and timing with no mid-frame pulse. A testbench timing error would also break frame 1. The upstream report of 1 failed check out of 4 in [evidence:prj-a0b3e1ca75.triage.e2] also argues against a wholesale testbench timing error, though I have not seen the content of that upstream testbench. (H3, rejected) Infrastructure, meaning a simulator or regression environment fault. The failure was reproduced outside the regression under icarus with a $finish reached normally [run:run-0002]. That points to a deterministic design-level behaviour rather than an environment fault. H3 would gain weight if the attached testbench passes on the platform run. (H4, rejected) Spec ambiguity. The design's own header states the ignore-while-busy rule without qualification. H4 would be revived only if a separate project specification, not provided to me, permits reloading while busy. Confidence note: VeriTriage classified the log only as unknown_failure at 30% confidence, with 'Likely RTL bug' as its top hypothesis [evidence:prj-a0b3e1ca75.triage.e1]. The link from the regression log to this specific mechanism comes from code reading. It is not yet confirmed by a run of the attached testbench."}], "files": [{"path": "tb/uart_tx_reload_while_busy_tb.v", "kind": "testbench", "title": "Directed repro: a start pulse while busy corrupts the frame in flight", "summary": "Self-checking Icarus-compatible testbench for the approved RTL [artifact:prj-a0b3e1ca75.design-under-investigation.a1], serving [requirement:req-d10c4df532]. It drives uart_tx with CLKS_PER_BIT=8 and has a UART receiver that samples near the middle of each bit on falling clock edges. It makes 4 checks: (1) frame 1, 0xA5 with no interference, decodes correctly with a valid stop bit; this is the control for the testbench-bug hypothesis; (2) frame 2, 0x5A with a one-cycle start pulse carrying data 0x0F during data bit 2, still decodes as 0x5A; (3) the frame 2 stop bit is high; (4) the line stays idle afterwards, so the ignored pulse does not launch a frame. On the RTL as written, I expect check 2 to fail. My reading predicts it will decode 0x0A. The testbench prints 'TEST FAILED' with a check tally and then calls $finish. It does the same on a watchdog timeout.", "entry": "uart_tx_reload_tb"}], "tool_runs": ["run-0001", "run-0002"], "claims": ["The root cause is the unconditional 'if (start) hold <= data;' that precedes the case statement in uart_tx_reload_while_busy.v. This is an inference from reading the code, not from a simulation I ran.", "On the RTL under investigation, the attached testbench is expected to fail exactly one check out of 4 (frame 2 data). The predicted value received is 0x0A instead of 0x5A. This has not been run by me. The platform's simulator.run with expect=fail will establish it.", "The failure in regression_fail.log is assumed to be the same mechanism as the one this testbench targets. I have not seen the regression log or the upstream testbench content."], "escalation": null, "notes": "No tool was run by me. run-0001 and run-0002 are listed only as the upstream runs this analysis relies on. The proposed fix (keep only the IDLE-branch load of hold) is a recommendation. It needs its own RTL change, a passing rerun of this testbench, and independent review (P6). If the platform run of this testbench passes on the approved RTL, H1 is weakened and the decision should be reopened. In that case, escalate to verification.debug.analysis.tech_lead."}
