arus runs, [run:run-0002] and [run:run-0003], both reproduced a single data-check failure. [run:run-0003] was refused only because the verdict line lacked the run nonce, not because the simulation misbehaved. (4) spec_ambiguity. Rejected because the design header states the ignore-while-busy behaviour explicitly. It would be refuted if an approved spec allowed reloading data while busy. Proposed fix, not applied here: delete the unconditional 'if (start) hold <= data;' and keep the load inside the IDLE branch only."}], "files": [], "tool_runs": ["run-0003"], "claims": ["The data bits sent after the mid-frame pulse in frame 2 come from the new data value (0x0F) rather than 0x5A. This is inferred from reading the RTL, not from a waveform.", "Removing the unconditional hold load is expected to make the directed test pass. This has not been simulated."], "escalation": {"reason": "The repro testbench must print a single verdict that carries the run's nonce. [run:run-0003] ([evidence:prj-a0b3e1ca75.root-cause.e1]) reproduced the functional failure but was rejected for 'no single verdict with this run's nonce'. None of the material given to me says how the nonce reaches the testbench (plusarg name, define or file) or what format the verdict line must use. Guessing would likely fail again, and adding extra verdict lines could break the 'single verdict' rule.", "question": "What is the platform's nonce and verdict contract for simulator.run testbenches? Specifically: how is the nonce delivered (for example a plusarg name or a `define), and what is the exact required verdict line format? Alternatively, can the verified upstream testbench uart_tx_triage_repro.v, which passed this check in run-0002, be made readable so I can copy its convention?", "options": ["Provide the nonce delivery mechanism and verdict line format; I will resubmit the directed testbench with one nonce-bearing verdict.", "Allow the verified upstream uart_tx_triage_repro.v to serve as the repro testbench for this task.", "Accept rtl_bug as the outcome on the existing evidence and waive the reproduction requirement at a human gate (P12)."]}, "notes": "Attempt 1's testbench reproduced the failure but printed 'TEST FAILED' without the run nonce, so the P9 gate was not met. I have not resubmitted a testbench because I cannot infer the nonce convention from the available sections, and a guess would probably be refused again. Once the contract is known, the existing directed testbench needs only one change: a single final verdict line in the required nonce format, with the watchdog's verdict made mutually exclusive with it. The analysis points strongly to rtl_bug; uncertainty mostly reflects the unmet reproduction and review requirements."}
